I do still really like the simplicity of taking a bitmap, either an `Int128` or a `Vector128<byte>`, that can be precomputed and stored in the binary.  However, there are a growing number of benefits to having some opaque data type instead:
- We can compute any number of bitmaps to accelerate different portions of the operation (scalar vs vectorized, potentially different maps if `Vector256<byte>` could be supported, etc)
- We can adapt the algorithm over time.  Exposing the actual bitmap means we're either locked to an algorithm optimized to that bitmap representation or every invocation needs to spend at least some amount of cycles converting it to something else.  And it seems likely that better algorithms will emerge, because folks think of new ways to do it and/or because new intrinsics become available that enable better approaches.
- We can special-case certain subsets of inputs if we come up with better ways to handle them, e.g. if all the inputs are in two consecutive ranges, if all the inputs are just the ASCII letters and we could do an xor and then a single-range comparison, etc.

On top of that, in theory it should be possible for Native AOT to still precompute everything and bake it into the binary; it already has some such capabilities, and if it doesn't already extend to whatever type we might employ here, it could extended to in the future.

Basically, in exchange for the simplicity of `Int128`/`Vector128<byte>` and slightly better startup _today_, we're giving up some amount of throughput on every use, potentially more throughput in the future if better algorithms are devised, and we're effectively hardcoding the algorithm being used into the public API.

